## 1. Critical Objective

Fix the existing YepStreamer platform so that every successfully uploaded and published video is stored persistently and remains available to all authorized users, regardless of who uploaded it.

The platform must behave like a centralized streaming service similar to Netflix and Crunchyroll.

**Mandatory rule: An uploaded video must never disappear simply because the uploader logs out, leaves the website, closes their browser, refreshes a page, or because the website is updated, redeployed, or restarted.**

Do not merely modify the frontend. Investigate and fix the complete upload-to-storage-to-database-to-publishing-to-playback workflow.

## 2. Diagnose and Fix the Existing Problem First

Before changing the website, inspect the current implementation and identify why videos disappear or are visible only to the uploader.

Check:

- Whether video files are stored in temporary server directories or browser memory.
- Whether uploaded files disappear after server restarts or application redeployments.
- Whether the database is using temporary, local, or nonpersistent storage.
- Whether video metadata is saved only in frontend state or local browser storage.
- Whether the uploaded video is linked to the uploader's private account instead of the shared catalog.
- Whether the public catalog query incorrectly filters videos by the current user's ID.
- Whether publication status is saved permanently in the database.
- Whether storage URLs expire or point to unavailable files.
- Whether frontend routes or environment changes break existing video references.
- Whether background cleanup jobs, retention policies, or storage lifecycle rules delete videos.
- Whether authentication rules prevent other authorized users from viewing published videos.
- Whether deployment configuration resets the database or overwrites persistent data.

Fix the underlying causes instead of adding temporary workarounds.

## 3. Permanent Original Video Storage

Store every successfully uploaded original video in durable, persistent object storage or an equivalent production-grade storage system.

Requirements:

- Never store the only copy of a video in browser memory, temporary folders, ephemeral containers, or temporary server filesystems.
- Keep original video files separate from application code and deployment artifacts.
- Use stable, unique asset IDs and persistent storage keys.
- Store file metadata, storage references, upload status, and publication status in a persistent database.
- Verify that the file has been fully uploaded and stored before marking the upload as successful.
- Use file integrity checks to detect incomplete or corrupted uploads.
- Preserve original files when generating thumbnails, subtitles, transcoded versions, or adaptive streaming renditions.
- Keep video storage independent of frontend rebuilds, application updates, and server restarts.
- Enable storage versioning, appropriate redundancy, backups, and tested recovery procedures.
- Do not automatically overwrite existing original files when a video with the same title is uploaded.

## 4. No Automatic Video Deletion

Implement a strict **No-Automatic-Deletion Policy**.

- Do not automatically delete or remove successfully uploaded video files.
- Do not delete videos when users log out, leave the website, close their browsers, or clear their browsing data.
- Do not delete video files when the application restarts, updates, redeploys, or changes its frontend.
- Do not remove originals after transcoding or thumbnail generation.
- Do not let routine maintenance or cache-clearing jobs delete original video assets.
- Disable storage lifecycle rules that would expire published originals or processed streaming assets unintentionally.
- Do not add ordinary delete-video or remove-video actions to the existing admin upload workflow.
- Prevent unauthorized backend services from deleting or overwriting stored originals.
- Keep videos that fail processing and allow the administrator to retry processing without re-uploading the original.
- If a file is temporarily unavailable, show a recoverable error and retain its database record and storage reference.
- Never silently remove catalog entries because of a temporary storage, network, or processing failure.

If a legal obligation requires content removal, handle it through a separately authorized compliance process with appropriate records and safeguards.

